body:
### Tested versions

v4.7.dev1.official [bf95b6258], v4.6.stable.official [89cea1439]

### System information

linux, amd, vulkan

### Issue description

I am developing a voxel game in Godot + Rust and ran into an interesting engine-related issue, as far as I understand.

I am using Vulkan rendering on AMD, and everything works perfectly. But two of my friends with Nvidia cards, a 3060 and a 3070, are experiencing rendering problems.

vulkan:

<img width="1920" height="1080" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e6ac8df7-fbb8-4062-a75f-7abca1fa9289" />

nvidia:

<img width="1280" height="592" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/64eac5b3-66bf-4b5b-b0cc-95ba336c190f" />

<img width="771" height="593" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9726de7f-44e1-40e8-b940-5057911d5aa5" />

opengl3:

<img width="1920" height="1080" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7fd61c0d-cb2a-4968-b703-f0232df2fad2" />

--

So, for rendering, I am using a clever approach where I generate the chunk shapes (meshes) using a binary greedy algorithm and then create a 3D texture to pass the block IDs for rendering.

It looks like this: 

<img width="1920" height="1080" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8d250d97-9e56-4517-975c-0f4d8849680c" />

In the shader, this will then be handled correctly at the stage:
```glsl
vec3 debug = (vec3(voxelCoord) + vec3(0.5)) / 32.0;
ALBEDO = debug;
```

That is, the fact that we see the grid is correct, and now we just need to figure out which block we need to draw.

I will show only the part of the shader where the problem is:
```glsl
uniform sampler2DArray blockDataArray: filter_nearest, repeat_disable, source_color;

void fragment() {
    ivec3 voxelCoord = ivec3(floor(localPos - faceDir * 0.5));
    voxelCoord = clamp(voxelCoord, ivec3(0), ivec3(31));

    vec4 idTexel = texelFetch(blockDataArray, ivec3(voxelCoord.xy, voxelCoord.z), 0);
    int blockId = int(floor(idTexel.r * 255.0 + 0.5));

    if (blockId == 0) {
        // bug
        vec3 debug = (vec3(voxelCoord) + vec3(0.5)) / 32.0;
        ALBEDO = debug;
    } else {
        // ok
```

Here you can see, as I mentioned earlier, that we will see the grid. But for my friends on Nvidia, blockId for some faces is 0 (air), and air is an error since there is no such block. That’s why they have rendering bugs.  

I tried to reproduce a similar situation and rolled back to OpenGL 3, and there the same effect occurs when the top face and -z are affected by the error.  

My texture is 3D and made with R8 (L8, RGB8, and other formats do not solve the problem). I also don’t use LODs.  

I found the cause – texelFetch. Or rather, I think it’s some mechanism behind it. I tried using texture() and textureLod(), but that didn’t help either. Switching to usampler2DArray didn’t help. And yes, before that I tried exactly the same thing with a 3D array, and the same problem occurred.  

It feels like on Nvidia and OpenGL 3, for some reason, it gets zeroed in texelFetch. At the same time, some faces render correctly, and we also know that in Vulkan everything is completely correct and there’s no issue in the image.  

So I think there is some bug.

### Steps to reproduce

I can't come up with an example for quick reproduction. Overall, I have described all the details very precisely, and there is nothing left to debug. The problem here is:
```glsl
vec4 idTexel = texelFetch(blockDataArray, ivec3(voxelCoord.xy, voxelCoord.z), 0);
int blockId = int(floor(idTexel.r * 255.0 + 0.5));
```

And it behaves differently on AMD vs Nvidia and Vulkan vs OpenGL3.

In the native fragment:
1
```
layout(set = 3, binding = 2) uniform highp texture2DArray m_blockDataArray;
```
2
```glsl
ivec3 m_voxelCoord = ivec3(floor((m_localPos - (m_faceDir * 0.5))));
m_voxelCoord = clamp(m_voxelCoord, ivec3(0,0,0), ivec3(31,31,31));
vec4 m_idTexel = texelFetch(sampler2DArray(m_blockDataArray, SAMPLER_NEAREST_CLAMP), ivec3(m_voxelCoord.xy, m_voxelCoord.z), 0);
int m_blockId = int(floor(((m_idTexel.r * 255.0) + 0.5)));
if ((m_blockId == 0))
{
    vec3 m_debug = ((vec3(m_voxelCoord) + vec3(0.5,0.5,0.5)) / 32.0);
    albedo_highp = m_debug;
}
```

But this is Vulkan. I don't know if it's even possible to view an OpenGL3 shader since it's already compiled in the cache.

### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)

I can't provide the project because it's a large game with quite a lot of code, and it's impossible to just extract a small renderer that takes up several thousand lines.
